{"id":"anti-example-001","type":"pattern","fact":"Example: NEVER use 'as any' type casting - it hides bugs and defeats TypeScript's type system.","recommendation":"Use proper type narrowing, generics, or 'as unknown as Type' for test mocks.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"documentation","reference":"TypeScript patterns guide","date":"2026-01-09"}],"tags":["typescript","type-safety"],"affectedFiles":["**/*.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-01-09T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
{"id":"antipattern-fixme-masks-secondary-bug","type":"gotcha","fact":"A `test.fixme` against a primary bug can mask a second, unrelated bug at a deeper layer. When the primary fix lands and the spec is un-fixme'd, the secondary bug surfaces as a 'new' failure even though it was always there. Example: #65's panel-render fixme on `home-code-runner-*` specs masked a separate WASM-runner-completion bug (now tracked at #70) — fixing the panel-render issue exposed it as a fresh failure.","recommendation":"When un-fixme'ing a spec, run it BEFORE declaring the un-fixme done. If it still fails, do not assume the gating fix was incomplete — diagnose whether a secondary, unrelated bug was masked. File a new tracking issue and re-fixme against the new issue rather than reverting the primary fix or claiming the spec passes.","confidence":"high","provenance":[{"source":"agent","reference":"Issue #65 WU2 — home-code-runner-execution.spec.ts revealed runner-completion bug after panel-render fix","date":"2026-04-30"}],"tags":["e2e","testing","fixme","debugging","bug-masking"],"affectedFiles":["e2e/specs/**/*.spec.ts"],"affectedServices":[],"createdAt":"2026-04-30T00:00:00Z","updatedAt":"2026-04-30T00:00:00Z","usageCount":0,"helpfulCount":0,"outdatedReports":0}
